 "verdict": "partly consistent: the first line and most of the second read as a simple letter-for-letter substitution (يا سيدا حاز احسانا ومكرمه وفضله شاع في السبع ... انعم بوعدك يا ... الكرام ويا صدر), but over repeated shapes only 88.2% take their majority letter. It breaks in two of my shape classes, which each hide two or three look-alike letter shapes: the lam-alif-like sign (م five times, ب three, ص once) and the alif-like sign (ف twice, م, ن); and in قمر البلاغه (24v l.9-10), where my transcription is short. 81 signs by eye against 89 letters; the last word المخادم has no signs after the final ا-group on 24v l.11. Needs a 2-3x pass on 24v l.8-11 separating the look-alike letter shapes.",
 "notes": [
  "Key as far as it holds (plain letter -> cipher letter shape): ا -> ع; ي -> م (and ٩ once); س -> و; د -> ٥; ح -> ف; ز -> ك; ن -> ل; و -> ط; م -> لا; ك -> ى; ر -> ﺲ; ه -> ص; ف -> ا; ض -> ٮ; ل -> ﻃ with a bar (and ﮭ); ش -> ٣; ع -> d-loop; ب -> a lam-alif-like shape; ق -> ﺳ. A letter-shape substitution: each plain letter is written as another letter.",
  "The record's count (88; segmenter forced on l.7) is 7 more than mine (81): the difference is in l.9-10 and possibly the margin numerals beside l.10-11 (a vertical note of digits, ٧٤٤٣٢, not counted here).",
  "Plaintext (25r l.1-2) not re-read on the master; الاقاليم is written above the line there, and the cipher has it in place (ع ﮭ ﺳ ع ﻃ م لا, with the alif after the lam missing)."
 ],
